@startuml block_io_stack
!theme plain
title Block Device I/O Stack

box "User Space" #LightBlue
  participant Userspace as "Application\n(dd, mount, etc)"
end box

box "VFS & Page Cache" #LightGreen
  participant VFS
  participant PageCache as "Page Cache"
end box

box "Block Layer" #LightYellow
  participant BIO as "bio layer"
  participant Scheduler as "I/O Scheduler"
  participant Queue as "Request\nQueue"
end box

box "Block Driver" #LightCoral
  participant Driver as "Driver\nmake_request()\nor request_fn()"
  participant Hardware as "Hardware\nController"
end box

== Write Flow ==
Userspace -> VFS: write()/writepage()
VFS -> PageCache: Cache page
PageCache -> BIO: Submit bio (write)
BIO -> Scheduler: bio -> request
Scheduler -> Queue: Enqueue request
Queue -> Driver: Invoke request handler

Driver -> Driver: Map request to sectors
Driver -> Hardware: Start DMA/I/O
Hardware --> Driver: Interrupt (completion)
Driver -> Queue: Signal completion

Queue --> Scheduler: ack
Scheduler --> BIO: complete bio
BIO --> PageCache: page written
PageCache --> VFS: ack
VFS --> Userspace: return bytes_written

== Read Flow ==
Userspace -> VFS: read()/readpage()
VFS -> PageCache: Check cache
alt Cache miss
  PageCache -> BIO: Submit bio (read)
  BIO -> Scheduler: bio -> request
  Scheduler -> Queue: Enqueue
  Queue -> Driver: Invoke request handler
  Driver -> Hardware: Start DMA read
  Hardware --> Driver: Interrupt (data ready)
  Driver -> Queue: Signal completion
  Queue --> Driver: Fill bio buffer
end
PageCache -> Userspace: Return page

note right of Driver
  Converts bio/requests to
  hardware-specific operations:
  - Map LBA sectors
  - DMA transfers
  - Interrupt handling
end note

@enduml
